-- SAYING "TAKE MY WORD FOR IT", AND BEING TOLD WHAT THAT COST.
--
--   ./tc examples/demo/Demo06Trust.thena
--
-- then, at the prompt:   :trust      :whnf nothingOwed      :whnf owed
--
-- `trusted T e` is a term whose type is ASSERTED: `e` is checked as usual, at
-- whatever type it really has, and the term is then given `T` with nothing
-- relating the two. What makes it worth having rather than an axiom is that it
-- REDUCES WHEN THE CLAIM COMES TRUE — so a term still printing as `trusted` is
-- a term whose claim is still load-bearing.
--
-- `:trust` sorts every claim into one of three: DISCHARGED (the two types are
-- convertible, so the trust carries nothing), PENDING (not met, but some
-- instance could still meet it), UNDISCHARGEABLE (it can never be met, by any
-- instance, in any later session).
--
-- A session starts at `no-undischargeable`, and that is asked at BOTH ways a
-- definition reaches the environment — `qed` and `declare` — after the kernel
-- has already accepted the term, because this is policy and not the kernel's
-- judgement.

module examples.demo.Demo06Trust where

import examples.demo.Demo01Expressions
import examples.demo.Demo02Judgments

open Ty
open Tm
open typing

-- A claim that is already true. It goes in without comment, and `:whnf` shows
-- that nothing is left of it: the claim was met, so the term contracted to its
-- body.
nothingOwed : typing`⊢ 0 : Nat`
nothingOwed = trusted typing`⊢ 0 : Nat` T-zero

-- One that is genuinely load-bearing. It claims the `T-succ` rule and offers
-- its own premise as the body, so nothing relates the two: `:whnf` still
-- prints `trusted`, and `:trust` lists it as PENDING.
--
-- THIS IS THE USEFUL SHAPE: state the lemma, get on with the theorem that
-- needs it, and come back. `:trust` is the list of what you still owe.
owed : forall (M : Tm) (d : typing`⊢ ${M} : Nat`) -> typing`⊢ ( succ ${M} ) : Nat`
owed = \ M d -> trusted typing`⊢ ( succ ${M} ) : Nat` d

-- …and here is the same statement with the debt paid off, for comparison. It
-- is the rule itself, so `:trust` says the file rests on nothing once the only
-- user of `owed` is this comment.
paid : forall (M : Tm) (d : typing`⊢ ${M} : Nat`) -> typing`⊢ ( succ ${M} ) : Nat`
paid = \ M d -> T-succ M d
